  "raw": "'s early work. Protestant leaders argued that people needed to be able to exercise their religious freedom and that it was the responsibility of the authorities to guarantee this freedom. In this sense, religious freedom in the United States is based on the idea that people do not have the right to religious domination.\n\nThe early leaders of the American Civil War were also active in promoting religious freedom. General Robert E. Lee expressed that religious freedom was necessary to prevent the civil war and it was the duty of Christians to defend religious freedom. General Sherman said that to make religion free and tolerable is to make ourselves free and tolerable and that religious freedom is one of the elements of freedom.\n\nThe First Amendment of the Constitution states, \"…Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof; or abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; or the right of the people peaceably to assemble, and to petition the government for a redress of grievances.\"",
  "serialized": "'s early work. Protestant leaders argued that people needed to be able to exercise their religious freedom and that it was the responsibility of the authorities to guarantee this freedom. In this sense, religious freedom in the United States is based on the idea that people do not have the right to religious domination. The early leaders of the American Civil War were also active in promoting religious freedom. General Robert E. Lee expressed that religious freedom was necessary to prevent the civil war and it was the duty of Christians to defend religious freedom. General Sherman said that to make religion free and tolerable is to make ourselves free and tolerable and that religious freedom is one of the elements of freedom. The First Amendment of the Constitution states, \"…Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion, or prohibiting the free exercise thereof; or abridging the freedom of speech, or of the press; or the right of the people peaceably to assemble, and to petition the government for a redress of grievances.\"",
